---
/**
 * /samdrea/vault/today — line 01. One day, two songs.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHAT THIS PAGE IS NOW, AND WHAT IT USED TO BE
 *
 * It used to be "song of the day, plus what she sent back". Structurally that was
 * a broadcast with an answer stapled underneath: my card first, at full size, with
 * her half rendered below a horizontal rule under the heading "what you sent
 * back". Both of us were posting a song a day even then — the framing just made
 * one of them secondary.
 *
 * A DAY IS NOW A PAIR. Two slots, side by side, same card template rendered twice
 * (see `slots` below — it is literally one loop, which is the only way to
 * guarantee neither side drifts into being the important one). Neither half waits
 * for the other, neither is an answer to the other, and the empty slot is the
 * ORDINARY case rather than an error: for most of a year exactly one of us will
 * have posted by the time the other opens this page.
 *
 * The empty slot is also where the action is. Hers holds the form; mine says, in
 * her language, that I have not picked one yet. No red, no "waiting", no word that
 * implies either of us is late.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE SESSION IS RE-VERIFIED HERE, AND THAT IS NOT THEATRE
 *
 * src/middleware.ts has already checked a signed, unexpired token for anything
 * under /samdrea/vault, or this render never happened. The check below is what
 * makes a routing mistake up there degrade to "she gets sent to the gate" instead
 * of "the internet gets her songs". If NEEDS_SESSION ever stops matching this
 * path, this page still refuses to render. Copied verbatim from the vault hub so
 * there is one pattern to audit rather than two.
 *
 * `session` specifically, NOT `session ?? admin`. My admin token cannot open this
 * page, which is a deliberate and slightly inconvenient choice: the vault is hers,
 * that is one rule, and one rule is worth more than the convenience of previewing
 * her page without answering the questions. /samdrea/dj renders its own preview
 * of the same pair for exactly this reason.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * IT READS THE STORE DIRECTLY, NOT ITS OWN API
 *
 * This page is server-rendered, so calling /api/us/song from here would mean the
 * server making an HTTP request to itself, forwarding her cookie, and paying a
 * round trip to reach a module it can just import. /api/us/song exists for the
 * client (see the rollover probe at the bottom); the page uses the store.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * IT WORKS WITH JAVASCRIPT OFF
 *
 * Every reaction and the posting form are real <form method="post"> elements that
 * 303 back here and re-render from the store. The script at the bottom upgrades
 * them to fetches so the page does not jump, and that is ALL it does. A
 * press-and-hold or a click-handler-on-a-div would have made the only path to
 * posting a scripted one; this way the enhancement is genuinely optional.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * REACTIONS GO BOTH WAYS, AND ONLY ONTO THE OTHER ONE'S CARD
 *
 * This used to be a bar of five captioned buttons hardcoded onto HIS card, whoever
 * was reading. Two faults, reported from a phone in one sentence: signed in as him
 * the only bar on the page was on his own song (which the endpoint refuses), and her
 * song could not be reacted to at all.
 *
 * Now every card that has a track shows one of two things, and never both:
 *
 *   * THE BAR — three one-tap emoji plus a free-choice input — on the OTHER one's
 *     card. `Slot.canReact`, which is per-slot and viewer-relative.
 *   * WHAT THEY SAID — the same emoji, read only, no buttons — on YOUR OWN card.
 *     You cannot react to your own song, so on your side they are a receipt.
 *
 * The prose captions ("on repeat", "okay, ow") are gone from the SCREEN and not from
 * the page: they are the buttons' aria-label now. Three buttons whose visible
 * content is aria-hidden decoration all announce as "button", so the words still have
 * to exist somewhere. See Reaction.name in kv.ts.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE ONE RULE
 *
 * The album art is the only warm, saturated thing on this page — on BOTH cards.
 * No filter, no tint, no blend mode, no overlay, and not on Spotify's embeds
 * either. The blue is light in the room around them. See the header of
 * public/css/us-song.css.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */
import { HER_NAME, SESSION_SECRET } from '../../../lib/us/config';
import { readCookie, verify } from '../../../lib/us/session';
import { touch } from '../../../lib/us/presence';
/* HER_TZ / HIS_TZ are the two LOCATIONS, and they are imported here for the same
   reason the clock strip on the hub imports them: a song's posted-at time is a fact
   about the poster's evening, and WING_TZ is not a place either of them has ever
   been. WING_TZ is still imported below — it is the CALENDAR, it still decides which
   day a song files under, and the rollover probe at the bottom of this file still
   asks it. The two are not interchangeable and this page now uses both, for the two
   different jobs. */
import { HER_TZ, HIS_TZ, identify, type Who } from '../../../lib/us/together';
import {
  MAX_REACTIONS_PER_DAY,
  MAX_REACTION_UNITS,
  WING_TZ,
  agoLabel,
  durationLabel,
  emptyPair,
  getExchange,
  isWingDate,
  reactionChips,
  reactionName,
  resurface,
  storeTier,
  summarize,
  wingDate,
  wingDateLabel,
  type DayPair,
  type ReactionChip,
  type Rhythm,
  type Side,
  type TrackRecord,
} from '../../../lib/us/kv';

export const prerender = false;

const secret = SESSION_SECRET();
const session = secret ? verify(secret, 'session', readCookie(Astro.cookies, 'session', Astro.url)) : null;

if (!session) return Astro.redirect('/samdrea', 302);

/* PRESENCE, WRITE ONLY. This page shows no presence line — a note about
   somebody else arriving would pull her out of the thing she came here to
   read. But time spent here is still time spent in the wing, so it stamps
   without reading. Without this, twenty minutes on this page would look
   like twenty minutes away, and the hub would tell him she had left.

   Never awaited for its value and never able to throw: presence.ts
   resolves to undefined on every failure path, including no Upstash. */
/* WHICH OF THEM IS READING. Was `_seenBy`, used only to stamp presence; the page
   now needs it for a second reason: whoever is reading gets the posting form for
   THEIR half. The gate is the only credential in the wing, so this comes from the
   whoami cookie via identify() — a label, not a permission. See whoami.ts. */
const viewer: Who = identify(Astro.cookies, Astro.url) ?? 'her';
const _seenBy = viewer;
await touch(_seenBy);

const her = HER_NAME();
const today = wingDate();

/** How many DAYS the archive renders. Matches ARCHIVE_LIMIT in /api/us/song. */
const ARCHIVE_LIMIT = 60;

/**
 * How far back the COUNTING goes. 365, which is also the ceiling getSongs()
 * enforces, so this is "everything there is" rather than an arbitrary window.
 *
 * Deliberately larger than ARCHIVE_LIMIT, and the reason is honesty: a total
 * computed over the sixty rows we happened to render would silently mean "sixty"
 * forever once the archive passed sixty days, and a number two people read as a
 * fact about themselves must not quietly be a fact about a page-size constant.
 *
 * The cost is real but small, and it is smallest on the tier that is actually
 * live: R2 stores the entire history in ONE JSON object, so reading 365 days costs
 * exactly what reading 60 does. On the Upstash tier it widens two MGETs and one
 * pipeline of HKEYS — still two round trips, more bytes. That is the price of a
 * true total and it is worth it.
 */
const HISTORY_WINDOW = 365;

/**
 * Everything, in one composite read, already folded into days.
 *
 * `todayPair` is whatever is filed under today's date — never "the most recent
 * day". Promoting yesterday would be a small lie that makes the whole feature
 * untrustworthy: she would have no way to tell a day I posted from a day I forgot,
 * and the honest empty slot is a better artifact than a quietly stale card.
 */
let pairs: DayPair[] = [];
let storeUnreachable = false;
try {
  ({ pairs } = await getExchange(HISTORY_WINDOW));
} catch (err) {
  console.error('[us] today page could not read the store:', err);
  storeUnreachable = true;
}

const todayPair = pairs.find((p) => p.date === today) ?? emptyPair(today);
/* Sliced here, not in the store call: getExchange's limit caps each SIDE's
   history, so the number of days it returns can exceed it when the two histories
   do not overlap. The counting below wants every day; only the rendering is
   capped. */
const archive = pairs.filter((p) => p.date !== today).slice(0, ARCHIVE_LIMIT);

/* WHICH SIDE THE READER IS. Drives one thing and one thing only on this page: you
   get the reaction bar on the OTHER card and never on your own.

   Derived here rather than compared inline at three call sites, because `viewer`
   speaks 'her'/'him' and a slot speaks 'hers'/'his' — two near-identical string
   unions, and a `viewer === slot.side` typo between them compiles fine and inverts
   the rule. One conversion, once. */
const viewerSide: Side = viewer === 'him' ? 'his' : 'hers';

/**
 * The numbers, derived in kv.ts rather than here.
 *
 * Same reason the calendar lives there: /samdrea/dj states the streak too, and
 * two implementations of "how many days in a row" would eventually disagree by one
 * with no way to tell which page was lying.
 */
const rhythm: Rhythm = summarize({ today, pairs });

/**
 * One old day, brought back — the whole day, both halves. Null when the archive is
 * too young for it to mean anything; see RESURFACE_MIN_AGE_DAYS in kv.ts.
 */
const again = resurface({ today, pairs });

/* Outcomes from the no-JavaScript reaction and posting paths, which arrive back
   here as a 303 with ?e=<code>. Written in her language, not in HTTP: she does not
   need to know what a 502 is, only that it was not her fault and it is worth
   another try. Both endpoints land here, so every message has to make sense for a
   tapped emoji AND for a pasted link. */
const errorCode = Astro.url.searchParams.get('e') ?? '';
const ERRORS: Record<string, string> = {
  store: "that didn't save — not your fault. try it again?",
  rate: 'okay, easy on the buttons. give it a minute.',
  unauthorized: 'your session ran out. answer the questions again and you are back in.',
  unconfigured: 'something is unplugged on my end. not your fault.',
  'bad-url': "that isn't a spotify TRACK link — use Share, then Copy link, from the song itself.",
  'bad-request': "the server couldn't read that. try again?",
  'bad-date': 'that day is not a real day.',
  'future-date': "that day hasn't happened yet.",
  /* The reaction path's own four. The vocabulary is open now, so `bad-reaction`
     ("that isn't one of the five") no longer describes anything that can happen —
     it is replaced rather than kept, because a stale sentence is worse than a
     generic one. Only a hand-made request reaches most of these, but a
     wrong-but-specific answer is cheaper to write than to diagnose later. */
  'bad-emoji': 'that needs to be one emoji on its own. one, and nothing else with it.',
  /* THE REPORTED BUG, NAMED. Reachable from the UI only if the identity flipped in
     another tab between render and submit, because the bar is not drawn on your own
     card at all. Says whose it is rather than "forbidden". */
  'own-song': "that one's yours. reactions go on the other one's song.",
  'bad-side': "that didn't say which song it was for. tap one of the buttons instead?",
  'too-many': `that song is holding all ${MAX_REACTIONS_PER_DAY} reactions it can. take one back first?`,
  'cross-site': 'that came from somewhere I do not recognise.',
  /* Only reachable if the identity switched between rendering the page and
     submitting it — e.g. flipped in another tab. Names the fix. */
  'not-your-half': 'that slot belongs to the other one of us. Yours is the other form.',
};
const errorText = errorCode ? (ERRORS[errorCode] ?? "that didn't go through. try again?") : '';

/* AND THE SUCCESS CODES, which this page did not have.

   /api/us/react 303s back here for BOTH outcomes, so with JavaScript switched off
   a stored tap and a refused one produced the same page: the chip state changed, or
   it didn't, and nothing said which. `?ok=` closes that. With the script on, the
   aria-live region does this job and the redirect never happens.

   Deliberately short. This is a confirmation, not an event log. */
const okCode = Astro.url.searchParams.get('ok') ?? '';
const OKS: Record<string, string> = {
  reacted: "got it. that's on the song now.",
  unreacted: 'taken back.',
};
const okText = okCode ? (OKS[okCode] ?? '') : '';

/* The posting form's success path: /api/us/reply 303s back with ?sent=<date>.
   The query parameter keeps its old name because the endpoint does — see that
   file's header on why the wire vocabulary and the product vocabulary diverge.

   VALIDATED BEFORE IT IS PRINTED, not merely escaped. Astro escapes it either way
   so there was never an injection here, but wingDateLabel() returns anything that
   is not a wing date verbatim — so an unvalidated value put whatever text was in
   the URL inside a green "posted" confirmation. A confirmation is the one place a
   page should not be repeating a stranger's words back. An invalid value now
   produces no banner at all, which is the honest answer: we cannot confirm a day
   that is not a day. */
const sentParam = Astro.url.searchParams.get('sent') ?? '';
const sentDate = isWingDate(sentParam) ? sentParam : '';

/**
 * `22:40` — the time on the POSTER'S OWN CLOCK. Empty when we never recorded one.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * IT USED TO SAY WING_TZ, AND WING_TZ IS NOBODY'S CLOCK
 *
 * This read `timeZone: WING_TZ` and printed `7:42 am`. WING_TZ is America/New_York,
 * which is the wing's CALENDAR — it decides which day a song files under so that a
 * song and a question agree about "today", and kv.ts explains at length why that
 * must be exactly one fixed zone. It is not a LOCATION. Neither of them lives in
 * New York, so every time on this page was wrong for both readers at once: his
 * 22:40 in Los Angeles read as `1:40 am`, and her 09:15 in Paris read as
 * `3:15 am`. Two people, nine hours apart, both being told about a third city.
 *
 * So the day heading still comes from the wing's calendar and the clock now comes
 * from the poster. Those are different facts and the card says them in different
 * words, one either side of the middle dot.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHICH ZONE, AND WHY THE FALLBACK IS A CONSTANT
 *
 * `tz` is captured from the poster's own device at post time (see the hidden field
 * on the forms below), so this stays right the first time either of them travels.
 * Every record written before that field existed has none, and so does every post
 * made with JavaScript off — for those, HER_TZ / HIS_TZ, which are correct today
 * and are the two lines together.ts already tells you to edit when she moves.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * SERVER-SIDE, AND h23
 *
 * Formatted here rather than in the browser, deliberately: a time rendered on the
 * client would be rendered against whichever clock is holding the phone, so the two
 * of them would read DIFFERENT strings for the same event and neither could quote it
 * to the other. And `hourCycle: 'h23'` because the wing is 24-hour everywhere on
 * purpose — see the comment on clocks() in together.ts. `en-GB` + h23 is the exact
 * pair clockOf() uses on the day page; one pattern, not two.
 *
 * The try/catch is not decoration. `tz` reaches here from a form field, and
 * Intl.DateTimeFormat throws RangeError on a name it does not know. kv.ts's
 * isTimeZone() has already rejected those on the way in and again on the way out,
 * so this is the third net — but a 500 on her vault over a decorative timestamp is
 * not a trade worth making, and no time at all is the correct rendering of a time we
 * cannot state.
 */
function postedAtLabel(ms: number, tz: string): string {
  if (!ms) return '';
  try {
    return new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-GB', {
      timeZone: tz,
      hour: '2-digit',
      minute: '2-digit',
      hourCycle: 'h23',
    }).format(new Date(ms));
  } catch {
    return '';
  }
}

/**
 * Where each of them is, when the record does not say. See together.ts, which calls
 * these the two lines to edit when she moves — and which is also why a stored `tz`
 * is better than either of them: it is right the moment either one travels, without
 * a deploy.
 */
const fallbackTz: Record<Side, string> = { his: HIS_TZ, hers: HER_TZ };

/**
 * The whole stamp for one card: `22:40`, and `22:40 their time` when the card is not
 * the reader's own.
 *
 * "THEIR TIME" ONLY ON THE OTHER PERSON'S CARD. Not a flourish — the alternative is
 * the exact bug day.astro already had and had fixed, where her own frame read
 * "04:11 their time" about herself. Nobody needs their own timezone named, and
 * naming it makes the page sound like it is talking about somebody else. On the
 * other card it is load-bearing: without it, two cards nine hours apart look like a
 * page that cannot add up.
 *
 * ONE FUNCTION FOR BOTH SIDES, called from the one card template, for the same
 * reason that template exists at all — a per-side copy is how one card ends up
 * reading its clock differently from the other.
 */
function stampFor(side: Side, track: TrackRecord): string {
  const at = postedAtLabel(track.postedAt, track.tz || fallbackTz[side]);
  if (!at) return '';
  return side === viewerSide ? at : `${at} their time`;
}

/**
 * Album, year, length — whichever of them we actually know.
 *
 * Returns an ARRAY rather than a joined string so the template can decide the
 * separator, and returns an EMPTY array rather than placeholders when nothing is
 * known. That is the whole contract of these fields: they exist only when the
 * Spotify Web API credentials do, so the entire pre-credential archive has none of
 * them and the correct rendering of "unknown" is no line at all — never "Unknown
 * album · —".
 */
function metaBits(track: TrackRecord): string[] {
  return [track.album ?? '', track.year ?? '', durationLabel(track.durationMs)].filter(Boolean);
}

/**
 * Reaction glyphs for one side of a day, plus the same thing in words.
 *
 * The list is already normalized and capped by kv.ts — every entry has been through
 * isReaction(), and this only joins them. `words` exists because the glyphs go
 * inside an aria-hidden span: an emoji row a screen reader cannot read is a row it
 * does not have, and reactionName() is the only place the naming rule lives.
 *
 * Rendered as TEXT by Astro at every call site, never with set:html. A stored emoji
 * is user input; validated user input is still user input.
 */
function reactionGlyphs(list: readonly string[]): { glyphs: string; words: string } {
  return {
    glyphs: list.join(' '),
    words: list.map(reactionName).join(', '),
  };
}

/* ============================================================================
   THE TWO SLOTS

   ONE ARRAY, ONE LOOP, ONE CARD TEMPLATE. This is the actual mechanism behind
   "both songs, equal weight" — not a pair of visually-matched blocks that a future
   edit could pull apart, but a single template that literally cannot render one
   side differently from the other except where this data says so.

   Everything that legitimately differs between the two halves is a STRING in
   here: whose it is, and what the slot says when it is empty. Everything else —
   the grid, the art treatment, the type, the player, the meta line — is shared by
   construction.

   The words are second person because this page is written to her: `me` is mine,
   `you` is hers. kv.ts's Side type stays 'his'/'hers' precisely so that the fixed
   identity and the page's voice are two different things, and /samdrea/dj can say
   "me" and "her" from the same records.
   ========================================================================= */

interface Slot {
  /** Fixed identity. Drives the CSS modifier and the reaction placement. */
  side: Side;
  /** The word on the card, in this page's voice. */
  who: string;
  track: TrackRecord | null;
  /** What the slot says when nobody has posted in it. Never "late", never red. */
  open: string;
  /** The quieter second line under that. */
  openSub: string;
  /** Shown in place of a title Spotify would not give us. */
  unnamed: string;
  /** The emoji already on THIS side's song today. Always an array. */
  given: string[];
  /**
   * May the reader leave a reaction here?
   *
   * FALSE ON THEIR OWN CARD, and that is the fix rather than a nicety. The bug was
   * a bar that only ever appeared on his song: signed in as him it was a row of
   * buttons the endpoint refused, and her song could not be reacted to at all. So
   * the bar is drawn on the OTHER one's card, both ways, and never on your own —
   * where a reaction would be a receipt you wrote yourself.
   */
  canReact: boolean;
  /** The buttons, when canReact. Built in kv.ts so the fold rules live once. */
  chips: ReactionChip[];
  /** Who left the reactions ON this card, in this page's voice. */
  fromWord: string;
}

/* WHOSE SIDE IS WHOSE, FROM THE READER'S SIDE.

   Every person label on this page used to be hardcoded in HIS voice — `who: 'me'`
   on the his slot, `who: 'you'` on hers, "I haven't picked one yet", "I sent" /
   "you sent" — because the page was written when the wing was one-directional and
   he was the only one who ever posted.

   Now both of them read it, and the labels never flipped. Verified against the live
   store with one song on each side: BOTH viewers saw the identical headings `me`
   and `you`, so when she opened it, HIS song was labelled "me" and hers "you". The
   data was always right; the words on top of it were his.

   So: your own side says "you", the other side says their name. The same scheme
   day.astro already uses for the photo frames, and for the same reason — "me" is
   only meaningful if you know whose voice the page is in, and now there are two. */
const theirWord = viewer === 'her' ? 'Sam' : (her || 'her');
const mineIsHis = viewer === 'him';

/* The word for each side, from the reader's side. Was computed inline twice in the
   slots below and twice more in minisFor(); reactions needed it a fifth and sixth
   time (whose reaction is on this card), which is one repetition too many for a
   label that must never disagree with itself. */
const whoWord: Record<Side, string> = {
  his: mineIsHis ? 'you' : theirWord,
  hers: mineIsHis ? theirWord : 'you',
};

/** A slot's empty-state sentences, told from the reader's side. */
function emptyFor(isMine: boolean): { open: string; openSub: string } {
  if (storeUnreachable) {
    /* BOTH SIDES HEDGE ON AN OUTAGE, and the reader's OWN side is the one that has
       to. getExchange() throwing yields an empty pair, so an unhedged slot would
       say "your side of today is open" and offer the form about a day they may
       already have posted on — the page stating a fact about the reader's own
       history that it cannot currently see. */
    return isMine
      ? {
          open: 'I can’t see your side from here.',
          openSub: 'If you already posted one, it’s still there. The shelf just isn’t answering.',
        }
      : {
          open: `I can’t see ${theirWord}’s side from here.`,
          openSub: 'There may well be one. The shelf just isn’t answering.',
        };
  }
  return isMine
    ? { open: 'Your side of today is open.', openSub: 'Whenever you feel like it.' }
    : { open: `Nothing from ${theirWord} yet today.`, openSub: 'Not yet, anyway.' };
}

/**
 * The reaction half of a slot. ONE function, called twice, for the same reason the
 * card is one template rendered twice: a per-side copy of this logic is how one card
 * ends up able to receive a reaction and the other does not, which is precisely the
 * bug being fixed.
 */
function reactionsFor(side: Side, track: TrackRecord | null) {
  const given = side === 'his' ? todayPair.reactions.onHis : todayPair.reactions.onHers;
  // A reaction needs a track to be about. `canReact` also stays false while the
  // store is unreachable, because the chips would be drawn from an empty pair and
  // every one of them would claim to be off.
  const canReact = Boolean(track) && side !== viewerSide && !storeUnreachable;
  return {
    given,
    canReact,
    chips: canReact ? reactionChips(given) : [],
    // Reactions on a side were left by the OTHER side — that is what makes them
    // mean something. See /api/us/react.
    fromWord: whoWord[side === 'his' ? 'hers' : 'his'],
  };
}

const slots: Slot[] = [
  {
    side: 'his',
    who: whoWord.his,
    track: todayPair.his,
    ...emptyFor(mineIsHis),
    unnamed: 'a song Spotify wouldn’t name',
    ...reactionsFor('his', todayPair.his),
  },
  {
    side: 'hers',
    who: whoWord.hers,
    track: todayPair.hers,
    /* BOTH SIDES HEDGE ON AN OUTAGE, and hers is the one that has to.
       When getExchange() throws, todayPair is emptyPair() — so an unhedged empty
       slot would tell her "your side of today is open", open the form and offer
       "pick one" about a day she may have already posted on. That is the page
       stating a fact about her own history that it cannot currently see, which is
       exactly the failure the store's read policy exists to avoid. It is also
       worse than the mirror-image case on my side: mine is a claim about me, hers
       is a claim about her, made to her. */
    ...emptyFor(!mineIsHis),
    unnamed: 'a song Spotify wouldn’t name',
    ...reactionsFor('hers', todayPair.hers),
  },
];

/* The disclosure and the button, which for the same reason must not claim she has
   not posted when we simply cannot tell. Computed here rather than inline so both
   of them come from ONE decision. */
const herSummary = storeUnreachable
  ? 'post one anyway'
  : todayPair.hers
    ? 'post a different one'
    : 'pick one';
const herSubmit = !storeUnreachable && todayPair.hers ? 'replace it' : 'post it';

/**
 * The stat strip.
 *
 * EVERY CELL IS CONDITIONAL, AND THAT IS THE REQUIREMENT RATHER THAN A FLOURISH.
 * A first-day page must not read "0 days · 0 in a row · I sent 0 · you sent 0".
 * Four zeroes is a page telling her this is empty and probably broken. So a cell
 * that would print a zero is not rendered at all, and when nothing qualifies the
 * whole strip disappears and the honest empty slots carry the page.
 *
 * ONE STREAK, AND IT IS THE SHARED ONE. There is deliberately no cell for "days I
 * posted in a row" alongside "days we both did": two streak numbers on one page
 * invites a comparison between two people who are not competing, and the smaller
 * mutual number is the one that measures the thing this feature is for. See
 * Rhythm.both in kv.ts.
 */
const stats: Array<{ label: string; value: string; hint?: string }> = [];
const since = rhythm.first ? `since ${wingDateLabel(rhythm.first, { withYear: true })}` : undefined;
if (rhythm.both > 0) {
  stats.push({
    label: 'both of us',
    value: String(rhythm.both),
    hint: since,
  });
} else if (rhythm.days > 0) {
  // Nothing mutual yet. Still say how long this has been going, because "day one"
  // and "day nine, and we keep missing each other" are different facts.
  stats.push({ label: rhythm.days === 1 ? 'day' : 'days', value: String(rhythm.days), hint: since });
}
if (rhythm.streak > 1) {
  // `> 1` and not `> 0`: "1 in a row" is not a streak, it is a Tuesday.
  stats.push({
    label: 'in a row',
    value: String(rhythm.streak),
    hint: rhythm.streakLive ? 'both of us' : 'through yesterday',
  });
}
/** Both sides get a cell, built by the same expression, or neither would. */
const sideCell = (label: string, count: number) =>
  count > 0
    ? {
        label,
        value: String(count),
        hint:
          rhythm.days > 1
            ? count === rhythm.days
              ? `all ${rhythm.days} days`
              : `of ${rhythm.days}`
            : undefined,
      }
    : null;
/* Also from the reader's side. `rhythm.his` is always Sam's count and
   `rhythm.hers` is always hers — the DATA is fixed; only the label flips. */
for (const cell of [
  sideCell(mineIsHis ? 'you sent' : `${theirWord} sent`, rhythm.his),
  sideCell(mineIsHis ? `${theirWord} sent` : 'you sent', rhythm.hers),
]) {
  if (cell) stats.push(cell);
}

/* ============================================================================
   ONE DAY, AS UP TO TWO SMALL LINKS

   Used by the archive AND by the resurfaced day, because they are the same thing
   at different sizes and two builders would eventually disagree about what a day
   looks like.

   Each side is its own <a>, and that is a capability the pair layout handed back:
   the old archive row was one big <a> wrapping everything, so her half could only
   ever be plain text inside it — an <a> inside an <a> is invalid HTML and browsers
   resolve it by dropping one, unpredictably. Two sibling links means both songs
   are reachable.
   ========================================================================= */

interface Mini {
  side: Side;
  who: string;
  track: TrackRecord;
  /** The reactions on THIS track. '' when there are none. */
  glyphs: string;
}

/**
 * BOTH SIDES CAN CARRY GLYPHS NOW.
 *
 * Her half used to hardcode `glyphs: ''` with a comment explaining that a reaction
 * on her own song "does not exist". It does now, and leaving that line would have
 * been the quietest possible version of this bug: the write path working, the
 * archive silently never showing it.
 */
function minisFor(pair: DayPair): Mini[] {
  const day = wingDateLabel(pair.date, { withYear: true });
  const out: Mini[] = [];

  /** One mini. Identical for the two sides except for the data — see the header. */
  const mini = (side: Side, track: TrackRecord, list: readonly string[]): Mini => {
    const { glyphs, words } = reactionGlyphs(list);
    return {
      side,
      /* Viewer-relative, like today's slots above. This block had the same
         hardcoded labels and would have shown her an old song of Sam's
         captioned "me". */
      who: whoWord[side],
      track,
      glyphs,
      label: [
        day,
        `${whoWord[side]}: ${track.title || 'a song Spotify wouldn’t name'}`,
        // Named by WHO LEFT IT, which is the other side. "you said" on a row of
        // reactions she did not write would be the page mis-attributing a tap.
        words ? `${whoWord[side === 'his' ? 'hers' : 'his']} said: ${words}` : '',
        'opens in Spotify',
      ]
        .filter(Boolean)
        .join('. '),
    };
  };

  if (pair.his) out.push(mini('his', pair.his, pair.reactions.onHis));
  if (pair.hers) out.push(mini('hers', pair.hers, pair.reactions.onHers));

  return out;
}
---

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0, viewport-fit=cover">
    <!-- viewport-fit=cover is REQUIRED for env(safe-area-inset-*) to be
         anything but 0. The pages set apple-mobile-web-app-status-bar-style
         to black-translucent, so iOS draws content UNDER the notch and the
         home indicator; without this attribute there is no opt-out AND no
         way to pad around it, so the studio's "exit" link sat behind the
         battery icon. The insets are applied in us.css and us-studio.css. -->
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="dark">
    <!-- Belt and braces: the middleware also sends X-Robots-Tag, which covers
         the redirects and JSON responses that cannot carry a meta tag. -->
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow, noarchive">
    <meta name="referrer" content="no-referrer">
    <title>[us] — one day, two songs</title>
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Space+Grotesk:wght@400;500;600;700&family=DM+Mono:wght@400;500&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
    <!-- Absolute paths: this page is at /samdrea/vault/today, so a relative
         href would resolve to /samdrea/vault/css/... and 404. us.css first —
         it owns every token us-song.css uses. -->
    <!-- Installable-app metadata. `scope` is "/" and that is a CORRECTION, not
         laziness. It was "/samdrea/", which looks tighter and quietly broke the
         installed app in three places: an installed PWA opens any out-of-scope
         URL in the BROWSER instead of the app, and "/samdrea" (the gate, where
         every expired session lands), "/api/us/out" (sign out) and all eleven
         "/api/us/*" form actions are every one of them outside "/samdrea/".
         So her session expiring would eject her to Safari to log back in, and
         with JavaScript off every form post would leave the app too. Nothing in
         the wing links to the public site, so a wide scope traps nothing.

         `start_url: /samdrea/vault` opens at the hub rather than the gate —
         she already holds a session cookie, so she skips the quiz on launch.
         `id` is pinned so the identity survives future manifest edits.

         DELIBERATELY NO SERVICE WORKER. Every page here is behind a session and
         several are per-day; a worker that cached the wrong response would serve
         her a stale room, or worse, hand a cached authorised page to whoever
         opened the app next. Standalone display, the icon and the black status
         bar all work without one. The only thing given up is offline, which for
         a room whose whole content is fetched per visit is not a real loss. -->
    <link rel="manifest" href="/manifest.webmanifest">
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/assets/us/icons/apple-touch-icon.png">
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes">
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-status-bar-style" content="black-translucent">
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-title" content="[us]">
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#000000">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/us.css">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/us-song.css">
    <link rel="icon" href="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%27http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%27%20viewBox%3D%270%200%2032%2032%27%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%2732%27%20height%3D%2732%27%20fill%3D%27%23000%27%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2716%27%20y%3D%2722.5%27%20font-family%3D%27Helvetica%2CArial%2Csans-serif%27%20font-size%3D%2716%27%20font-weight%3D%27700%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%3E%3Ctspan%20fill%3D%27%233e7bff%27%3E%5B%3C%2Ftspan%3E%3Ctspan%20fill%3D%27%23fff%27%3Eus%3C%2Ftspan%3E%3Ctspan%20fill%3D%27%233e7bff%27%3E%5D%3C%2Ftspan%3E%3C%2Ftext%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E">
</head>
<body class="studio">
<div class="studio-wrap">

    <p class="studio-kicker">01 &middot; one day, two songs</p>

    <h1 class="us-mark">
        <span class="us-mark-bracket" aria-hidden="true">[</span>today<span
              class="us-mark-bracket" aria-hidden="true">]</span>
    </h1>

    {errorText && (
        <p class="notice" data-kind="bad" role="status">hm &mdash; {errorText}</p>
    )}

    {sentDate && !errorText && (
        <p class="notice" data-kind="ok" role="status">
            posted &mdash; that's yours for {wingDateLabel(sentDate, { withYear: true })}. I'll see it.
        </p>
    )}

    {/* The no-JavaScript reaction path's confirmation. Only ever seen with the
        script off, because the enhanced path never navigates. Suppressed when
        there is an error, so the page never shows both at once. */}
    {okText && !errorText && (
        <p class="notice" data-kind="ok" role="status">{okText}</p>
    )}

    {storeUnreachable && (
        <p class="notice" data-kind="bad" role="status">
            hm &mdash; I cannot reach the shelf where these live. Nothing is lost,
            it just is not answering right now. Try again in a minute.
        </p>
    )}

    {/* Dev only. She should never be shown a sentence about serverless
        instances; on my own page (/samdrea/dj) the same warning is permanent,
        because there it is actionable. */}
    {import.meta.env.DEV && storeTier() === 'memory' && (
        <p class="notice" data-kind="warn">
            dev &mdash; in-process store. Non-durable, per-instance.
        </p>
    )}

    {/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
        THE STAT STRIP

        A <dl>, because these are label/value pairs and that is what a
        description list is for — a row of <div>s would read to a screen
        reader as four unlabelled numbers. The <div> wrappers inside are
        valid HTML5 dl grouping and are what make each pair one grid cell.

        Every number here is COUNTED from stored records by summarize() in
        kv.ts. Nothing is estimated, nothing is rounded, and a cell that
        would say zero is absent instead. See the `stats` builder above.
        ------------------------------------------------------------------ */}
    {stats.length > 0 && (
        <dl class="rhythm">
            {stats.map((cell) => (
                <div class="rhythm-cell">
                    <dt class="rhythm-label">{cell.label}</dt>
                    <dd class="rhythm-value">
                        {cell.value}
                        {cell.hint && <span class="rhythm-hint">{cell.hint}</span>}
                    </dd>
                </div>
            ))}
        </dl>
    )}

    {/* ==================================================================
        THE DAY

        Two slots from one template. The <section> is labelled rather than
        headed, because the <h1> above already says which day this is and a
        second heading saying it again is noise in the outline.

        Source order is his slot then hers, and on a phone that is also
        reading order — the one place any sequence exists between the two.
        It is why every card names its side IN WORDS: nothing here depends
        on position or on colour.
        ================================================================== */}
    <section class="pair" aria-label={`both sides of ${wingDateLabel(today, { withYear: true })}`}>
        {slots.map((slot) => (
            <article
                class={`song song--${slot.side}`}
                aria-labelledby={`who-${slot.side}`}
                data-open={slot.track ? undefined : 'true'}
            >
                {/* The <h2> is the side. Both halves are therefore peers in
                    the document outline as well as on screen, which is the
                    same claim the layout makes, made where a screen reader
                    can hear it. The track title is the <h3> under it. */}
                <h2 class="song-side" id={`who-${slot.side}`}>{slot.who}</h2>

                {slot.track ? (
                    <>
                        <div class="song-head">
                            <span class="song-frame">
                                {slot.track.art ? (
                                    /* alt="" on purpose: the title and artist are
                                       right there as real text, so alt text would
                                       make a screen reader say the same thing
                                       twice. This is a decorative duplicate of
                                       adjacent content, which is exactly the case
                                       empty alt exists for.

                                       referrerpolicy="no-referrer" matches the
                                       wing's Referrer-Policy header — Spotify's
                                       CDN has no business learning the URL she is
                                       reading this on. */
                                    <img
                                        class="song-art"
                                        src={slot.track.art}
                                        alt=""
                                        width="320"
                                        height="320"
                                        loading="lazy"
                                        decoding="async"
                                        referrerpolicy="no-referrer"
                                    >
                                ) : (
                                    <span class="song-art--empty" aria-hidden="true">[ ]</span>
                                )}
                            </span>

                            <div class="song-body">
                                {/* The day is on BOTH cards, and yes that repeats
                                    it. Deliberate: each card is self-contained,
                                    the same template renders it on /samdrea/dj
                                    where there is no [today] heading above it,
                                    and someone arriving at the second <article>
                                    by keyboard or screen reader should not have to
                                    remember which day this was. The time after it
                                    is the part that actually differs.

                                    TWO DIFFERENT CLOCKS, EITHER SIDE OF THE DOT.
                                    The date is the WING's calendar day — the thing
                                    that decides which page a song lands on. The
                                    time is the POSTER's own wall clock. Reading
                                    them as one phrase is a bit of a cheat and it
                                    is the right cheat: "Sun 23 Aug · 22:40" is
                                    how a person would say it, and the alternative
                                    (naming New York) put a third city on a page
                                    about two people. See stampFor(). */}
                                <p class="song-day">
                                    {wingDateLabel(slot.track.date)}
                                    {stampFor(slot.side, slot.track) && (
                                        <> &middot; {stampFor(slot.side, slot.track)}</>
                                    )}
                                </p>

                                {slot.track.title ? (
                                    <h3 class="song-title">{slot.track.title}</h3>
                                ) : (
                                    /* Spotify's metadata call failed. The embed
                                       below still knows what the track is, so this
                                       says so plainly rather than rendering an
                                       empty heading. */
                                    <h3 class="song-title song-title--unknown">{slot.unnamed}</h3>
                                )}

                                {slot.track.artist && <p class="song-artist">{slot.track.artist}</p>}

                                {/* Album, year, length. Rendered ONLY for what we
                                    actually know: these three come from the
                                    Spotify Web API, which needs credentials that
                                    may not exist, so on the whole pre-credential
                                    archive metaBits() returns [] and this line is
                                    simply not there. A "—" placeholder would be a
                                    worse answer than silence, and an "Unknown
                                    album" label would be a lie with a straight
                                    face.

                                    <ul> rather than one string because a screen
                                    reader should read three facts as three facts.
                                    The separator is a CSS ::before on the second
                                    and later items, so it is never announced. */}
                                {metaBits(slot.track).length > 0 && (
                                    <ul class="song-meta">
                                        {metaBits(slot.track).map((bit) => <li>{bit}</li>)}
                                    </ul>
                                )}
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        {/* Both sides get the same placeholder, from the same
                            branch. A per-side wording here would be a difference
                            with no reason behind it, and every reasonless
                            difference between the two cards is a step back
                            towards one of them being the main one. */}
                        {slot.track.note ? (
                            <p class="song-note">{slot.track.note}</p>
                        ) : (
                            <p class="song-note song-note--empty">No note. Just the song.</p>
                        )}

                        {/* The official embed, on BOTH cards. `id` has been
                            through /^[A-Za-z0-9]{22}$/ twice — once when it was
                            pasted, once when it was read back out of the store —
                            before it gets near this src, which is the only reason
                            interpolating it here is safe.

                            `allow` deliberately omits autoplay: two embeds on one
                            page that could both start on their own is a page that
                            ambushes you, and the play buttons are right there. */}
                        <div class="song-player">
                            <iframe
                                src={`https://open.spotify.com/embed/track/${slot.track.id}`}
                                title={slot.track.title
                                    ? `Spotify player: ${slot.track.title}`
                                    : 'Spotify player'}
                                width="100%"
                                height="152"
                                loading="lazy"
                                allow="encrypted-media; clipboard-write; fullscreen; picture-in-picture"
                                referrerpolicy="no-referrer"
                            ></iframe>
                        </div>

                        <p class="song-links">
                            <a
                                href={`https://open.spotify.com/track/${slot.track.id}`}
                                target="_blank"
                                rel="noreferrer noopener"
                            >open in spotify &nearr;</a>
                        </p>
                    </>
                ) : (
                    /* AN OPEN SLOT. Not an error, not a warning, not a
                       countdown — see the CSS for `.song[data-open]`. The
                       second sentence carries the honest detail so the first
                       one does not have to hedge. */
                    <p class="song-open">
                        {slot.open}
                        <span class="song-open-sub">{slot.openSub}</span>
                    </p>
                )}

                {/* --------------------------------------------------------
                    REACTIONS — on the OTHER one's card, on the song they
                    belong to.

                    WHAT THIS BLOCK USED TO BE, AND WHY IT WAS THE BUG.

                    It was gated `slot.side === 'his' && slot.track` and
                    nothing else. So the bar appeared on HIS card whoever was
                    reading: signed in as him it was a row of buttons the
                    endpoint refused with a 403, and her song had no bar at
                    all. Reported from a phone as "I can only react to Sam's
                    songs, even if I'm logged in as Sam" — two faults in one
                    sentence, and both of them this line.

                    Now the gate is `canReact`, which is per-slot and
                    viewer-relative: the other one's card, never your own.
                    See reactionsFor() above and /api/us/react's header for
                    why your own song is refused rather than allowed.

                    THE PROSE LABELS ARE GONE FROM THE SCREEN AND NOT FROM
                    THE PAGE. Each button used to carry "on repeat" / "okay,
                    ow" as visible text under the glyph, which is what the
                    owner asked to lose. The words now live in aria-label
                    only — because a button whose entire content is
                    aria-hidden decoration announces as "button", and three
                    of those in a row is a bar a screen reader cannot use.
                    Invisible to the eye, present to a screen reader; that
                    distinction is the whole reason `name` still exists in
                    kv.ts.

                    Sibling forms, one button each. Not one form with several
                    buttons: which button submitted a multi-button form
                    depends on name/value pairs that a keyboard Enter
                    resolves differently across engines, and that is not a
                    thing worth being clever about.

                    The hidden `on` field carries the OPPOSITE of the current
                    state, so a submit is always a toggle and the server is
                    told the desired end state rather than an action. That is
                    what makes the endpoint idempotent: a double-tap, a retry
                    and a resubmitted form all land on the same value.

                    `side` is hidden here and CHECKED against the cookie at
                    the endpoint. It is in the body because it is a fact about
                    the page, not about the caller; editing it in devtools
                    buys a 403 and nothing else.
                    -------------------------------------------------------- */}
                {slot.canReact && (
                    <>
                        <fieldset class="react-bar">
                            <legend class="react-legend">did you hear it?</legend>
                            <div class="react-row">
                                {slot.chips.map((chip) => (
                                    <form class="react-form" method="post" action="/api/us/react">
                                        <input type="hidden" name="date" value={todayPair.date}>
                                        <input type="hidden" name="side" value={slot.side}>
                                        <input type="hidden" name="reaction" value={chip.emoji}>
                                        <input type="hidden" name="on" value={chip.on ? '0' : '1'}>
                                        {/* aria-label carries the name; aria-pressed
                                            carries the state. The glyph is
                                            aria-hidden because it is the same
                                            information in a form the label already
                                            says better. */}
                                        <button
                                            class="react"
                                            type="submit"
                                            aria-pressed={chip.on ? 'true' : 'false'}
                                            aria-label={chip.name}
                                            title={chip.name}
                                        >
                                            <span class="react-emoji" aria-hidden="true">{chip.emoji}</span>
                                        </button>
                                    </form>
                                ))}
                            </div>

                            {/* ------------------------------------------------
                                ANY EMOJI AT ALL — a real form, not a picker.

                                A full grid of every emoji is a lot of surface
                                for a thing whose job is "say the one you
                                meant", and every version of it needs
                                JavaScript. The phone already HAS an emoji
                                picker: it is the key next to the space bar. So
                                this is one text input, and it works with
                                scripting off because it is a form.

                                DELIBERATELY NOT `maxlength` ALONE. The cap
                                here is a courtesy so the field cannot be
                                filled with a paragraph; the actual rule is
                                isReaction() on the server, which requires
                                exactly one grapheme cluster and requires it to
                                be an emoji. Nothing about this input is
                                trusted — see /api/us/react.

                                No hidden `on`: absent means true at the
                                endpoint (see readOn), so this form adds and
                                the chips it creates are what take it back.
                                ------------------------------------------------ */}
                            <form class="react-any" method="post" action="/api/us/react">
                                <input type="hidden" name="date" value={todayPair.date}>
                                <input type="hidden" name="side" value={slot.side}>
                                <label class="react-any-label" for={`react-any-${slot.side}`}>
                                    or any emoji
                                </label>
                                <div class="react-any-row">
                                    <input
                                        class="react-any-input"
                                        id={`react-any-${slot.side}`}
                                        name="reaction"
                                        type="text"
                                        autocomplete="off"
                                        autocapitalize="none"
                                        autocorrect="off"
                                        spellcheck="false"
                                        maxlength={MAX_REACTION_UNITS}
                                        size="2"
                                        aria-describedby={`react-any-hint-${slot.side}`}
                                        required
                                    >
                                    <button class="react-any-submit" type="submit">add</button>
                                </div>
                                <p class="react-any-hint" id={`react-any-hint-${slot.side}`}>
                                    One emoji, from your keyboard&rsquo;s emoji key. Up to
                                    {' '}{MAX_REACTIONS_PER_DAY} on one song.
                                </p>
                            </form>
                        </fieldset>

                        {/* Where the script announces the result of a tap.
                            Visually hidden because the button's own pressed
                            state is the visible feedback; this exists so that
                            state change is also SPOKEN.

                            EXACTLY ONE OF THESE ON THE PAGE, and that is a
                            property of `canReact` rather than a coincidence:
                            you can react to one card and never to both, so the
                            branch that renders this id runs once. */}
                        <p class="sr-only" id="react-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
                    </>
                )}

                {/* --------------------------------------------------------
                    AND ON YOUR OWN CARD: WHAT THEY SAID, READ ONLY.

                    Not buttons. You cannot react to your own song, so the
                    same emoji that are a control on their card are a receipt
                    on yours — which is the whole point of the feature and was
                    previously impossible to see from one of the two sides.

                    Absent entirely when there are none, rather than rendered
                    as an empty row explaining its own emptiness.

                    Every glyph is aria-hidden with the name beside it in an
                    sr-only span, for the same reason as the buttons above: an
                    emoji is not a label, and a list of them read aloud as
                    nothing is a list that is not there.
                    -------------------------------------------------------- */}
                {slot.track && !slot.canReact && slot.given.length > 0 && (
                    <div class="react-bar">
                        <p class="react-legend">{slot.fromWord} said</p>
                        <ul class="react-given">
                            {slot.given.map((emoji) => (
                                <li class="react-given-item">
                                    <span class="react-emoji" aria-hidden="true">{emoji}</span>
                                    <span class="sr-only">{reactionName(emoji)}</span>
                                </li>
                            ))}
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                )}

                {/* --------------------------------------------------------
                    HER FORM — inside HER slot, which is the point.

                    On a day she has not posted, the empty half of the page is
                    the thing she can act on rather than a gap with a form
                    somewhere underneath it. On a day she has, it collapses to
                    one line under her own card.

                    A real <form method="post" action="/api/us/reply"> that
                    303s back here, exactly like the reaction forms. It works
                    with JavaScript switched off; the script at the bottom
                    upgrades it to a fetch for ONE reason that actually
                    matters — a native submit that fails has already navigated
                    away and taken the note she just typed with it, and
                    retyping a paragraph on a phone is the difference between
                    doing this and not.

                    <details> rather than two markup paths: `open` when she
                    has not posted yet, closed once she has. It collapses
                    natively with no JavaScript at all.

                    THERE IS EXACTLY ONE OF THESE ON THE PAGE, which is why it
                    lives here rather than in both branches of the ternary
                    above: two copies would mean two ids and a script that has
                    to guess which one it is wired to.

                    The hidden `date` is this day, and the server validates it
                    anyway — it is a primary key and this page is not trusted
                    to have got it right.
                    -------------------------------------------------------- */}
                {/* HIS HALF, POSTABLE FROM HERE.
                    It used to live on /samdrea/dj behind a second credential, for
                    no reason except the order things were built: the site started
                    one-directional and his end never moved when it went mutual. So
                    "one day, two songs" was split across two pages and needed two
                    logins.

                    Gated on the VIEWER, not just the side — she is shown his card
                    but never his form, because a form she can see and never use is
                    worse than no form. Same endpoint, same fields, same caps as
                    hers; /api/us/song now takes a session like every other write
                    and reads `who` from the cookie rather than the body. */}
                {slot.side === 'his' && viewer === 'him' && (
                    <details class="reply-details" open={!slot.track && !storeUnreachable}>
                        <summary class="reply-summary">
                            {slot.track ? 'swap yours for another one' : 'pick yours'}
                        </summary>

                        <form class="dj-form" id="mine-form" method="post" action="/api/us/song">
                            <input type="hidden" name="date" value={todayPair.date}>
                            {/* WHERE HE IS, at the moment he posts. Filled in by the
                                script at the bottom from
                                Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone,
                                which on an iPhone is the timezone iOS itself is set
                                to — so this follows him when he travels and no line
                                of source has to be edited.

                                SHIPS EMPTY, and stays empty with JavaScript off.
                                That is a supported state, not a degraded one: the
                                endpoint drops a blank without complaining and the
                                card falls back to HIS_TZ. Capturing a location is
                                worth having; it is not worth making the
                                no-JavaScript path fail over.

                                Reading it here rather than sending the browser's
                                minutes-from-UTC is deliberate — an offset is right
                                today and wrong after the next DST change, and the
                                zone NAME is the thing Intl can reason about. */}
                            <input type="hidden" name="tz" value="" data-tz>

                            <div class="dj-field">
                                <label class="dj-label" for="mine-url">Spotify link</label>
                                {/* type="text" with inputmode="url", NOT type="url":
                                    the native validator rejects `spotify:track:...`,
                                    which the endpoint accepts. */}
                                <input
                                    class="dj-input"
                                    id="mine-url"
                                    name="url"
                                    type="text"
                                    inputmode="url"
                                    autocomplete="off"
                                    autocapitalize="none"
                                    autocorrect="off"
                                    spellcheck="false"
                                    placeholder="open.spotify.com/track/..."
                                    aria-describedby="mine-url-hint"
                                    required
                                >
                                <p class="dj-hint" id="mine-url-hint">
                                    Share &rarr; Copy link, from the song itself. Albums and playlists
                                    won&rsquo;t work.
                                </p>
                            </div>

                            <div class="dj-field">
                                <label class="dj-label" for="mine-note">
                                    Your note <span class="dj-opt">&mdash; why this one</span>
                                </label>
                                <textarea
                                    class="dj-textarea"
                                    id="mine-note"
                                    name="note"
                                    rows="4"
                                    maxlength="600"
                                ></textarea>
                            </div>

                            <div class="dj-field">
                                <label class="dj-label" for="mine-artist">
                                    Artist <span class="dj-opt">&mdash; optional</span>
                                </label>
                                <input
                                    class="dj-input"
                                    id="mine-artist"
                                    name="artist"
                                    type="text"
                                    maxlength="120"
                                    autocapitalize="words"
                                    aria-describedby="mine-artist-hint"
                                >
                                <p class="dj-hint" id="mine-artist-hint">
                                    Spotify only gives us the title and the art. Leave it blank and
                                    we&rsquo;ll skip that line.
                                </p>
                            </div>

                            <button class="dj-submit" id="mine-submit" type="submit">
                                {slot.track ? 'swap it' : 'post it'}
                            </button>
                            <p class="notice" id="mine-status" role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden></p>
                        </form>
                    </details>
                )}

                {/* AND GATED ON THE VIEWER TOO, mirroring his above. Without
                    this he was shown HER posting form as well as his own — each of
                    them posts their own half, and /api/us/reply would refuse him
                    anyway, so it was a form he could see and never use. */}
                {slot.side === 'hers' && viewer === 'her' && (
                    <details class="reply-details" open={!slot.track && !storeUnreachable}>
                        <summary class="reply-summary">{herSummary}</summary>

                        <form class="dj-form" id="reply-form" method="post" action="/api/us/reply">
                            <input type="hidden" name="date" value={todayPair.date}>
                            {/* WHERE SHE IS, at the moment she posts. Same field,
                                same script, same shipped-empty behaviour as the one
                                on his form above — this is the half that actually
                                moves, since she is the one who relocated. */}
                            <input type="hidden" name="tz" value="" data-tz>

                            <div class="dj-field">
                                <label class="dj-label" for="reply-url">Spotify link</label>
                                {/* type="text" with inputmode="url", NOT
                                    type="url": the native URL validator rejects
                                    `spotify:track:...`, which the endpoint
                                    accepts, so type="url" would block a valid
                                    paste before it ever reached the server. */}
                                <input
                                    class="dj-input"
                                    id="reply-url"
                                    name="url"
                                    type="text"
                                    inputmode="url"
                                    autocomplete="off"
                                    autocapitalize="none"
                                    autocorrect="off"
                                    spellcheck="false"
                                    placeholder="open.spotify.com/track/..."
                                    aria-describedby="reply-url-hint"
                                    required
                                >
                                <p class="dj-hint" id="reply-url-hint">
                                    Share &rarr; Copy link, from the song itself. Albums and playlists
                                    won&rsquo;t work.
                                </p>
                            </div>

                            <div class="dj-field">
                                <label class="dj-label" for="reply-note">
                                    Your note <span class="dj-opt">&mdash; why this one</span>
                                </label>
                                {/* maxlength matches MAX_NOTE in the endpoint,
                                    which is the SAME cap as mine. The browser
                                    stopping her at 600 is friendlier than the
                                    server silently truncating what she wrote. */}
                                <textarea
                                    class="dj-textarea"
                                    id="reply-note"
                                    name="note"
                                    rows="4"
                                    maxlength="600"
                                ></textarea>
                            </div>

                            {/* Optional, and new: her card used to have no way to
                                carry an artist at all, so on the credential-free
                                metadata path her songs rendered one line poorer
                                than mine every single day. Same field, same cap,
                                same optional treatment as the one on my form. */}
                            <div class="dj-field">
                                <label class="dj-label" for="reply-artist">
                                    Artist <span class="dj-opt">&mdash; optional</span>
                                </label>
                                <input
                                    class="dj-input"
                                    id="reply-artist"
                                    name="artist"
                                    type="text"
                                    maxlength="120"
                                    autocapitalize="words"
                                    aria-describedby="reply-artist-hint"
                                >
                                <p class="dj-hint" id="reply-artist-hint">
                                    Spotify only gives us the title and the art. Leave it blank and
                                    we&rsquo;ll skip that line.
                                </p>
                            </div>

                            <button class="dj-submit" id="reply-submit" type="submit">{herSubmit}</button>

                            {/* The enhancement script's channel. aria-live so the
                                outcome is announced without moving focus; hidden
                                until there is something to say. */}
                            <p class="notice" id="reply-status" role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden></p>
                        </form>
                    </details>
                )}
            </article>
        ))}
    </section>

    {/* ==================================================================
        ONE OLD DAY, BROUGHT BACK

        The whole day, both halves — the old version resurfaced one of my
        songs and structurally could not bring back hers, which for a
        memory feature is the wrong thing to be incapable of.

        Absent entirely until the archive is old enough for it to mean
        something (see RESURFACE_MIN_AGE_DAYS in kv.ts), which is also why
        there is no empty state for it: a "nothing to resurface yet" box
        would be a box explaining its own absence.

        Links, not embeds. Sixty players on a page is a page that never
        finishes loading, and this is the same reasoning that keeps iframes
        out of the archive below.
        ================================================================== */}
    {again && (
        <>
            <hr class="studio-rule">

            <p class="song-eyebrow">worth playing again</p>

            <div class="again">
                <p class="archive-date">
                    {wingDateLabel(again.date, { withYear: true })} &middot; {agoLabel(again.date, today)}
                </p>
                <div class="archive-tracks">
                    {minisFor(again).map((m) => (
                        <a
                            class="mini"
                            href={`https://open.spotify.com/track/${m.track.id}`}
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noreferrer noopener"
                            aria-label={m.label}
                        >
                            {m.track.art ? (
                                <img
                                    class="mini-thumb"
                                    src={m.track.art}
                                    alt=""
                                    width="56"
                                    height="56"
                                    loading="lazy"
                                    decoding="async"
                                    referrerpolicy="no-referrer"
                                >
                            ) : (
                                <span class="mini-thumb--empty" aria-hidden="true">[ ]</span>
                            )}
                            {/* aria-hidden throughout: every word below is
                                already in this link's aria-label, and hearing
                                it twice is worse than not hearing it once. */}
                            <span class="mini-body" aria-hidden="true">
                                <span class="mini-side">{m.who}</span>
                                <span class="mini-title">{m.track.title || 'a song with no name'}</span>
                                {m.track.note && <span class="mini-note">{m.track.note}</span>}
                                {m.glyphs && <span class="mini-reacts">{m.glyphs}</span>}
                            </span>
                        </a>
                    ))}
                </div>
            </div>
        </>
    )}

    <hr class="studio-rule">

    <p class="song-eyebrow">every day before this one</p>

    {archive.length > 0 ? (
        <ul class="archive">
            {archive.map((pair) => (
                <li class="archive-row">
                    {/* The day, once, for the whole row. Not repeated on each
                        track — it is the same day, and saying it twice is how
                        a ledger stops being readable. */}
                    <p class="archive-date">{wingDateLabel(pair.date, { withYear: true })}</p>
                    <div class="archive-tracks">
                        {minisFor(pair).map((m) => (
                            <a
                                class="mini"
                                href={`https://open.spotify.com/track/${m.track.id}`}
                                target="_blank"
                                rel="noreferrer noopener"
                                aria-label={m.label}
                            >
                                {m.track.art ? (
                                    <img
                                        class="mini-thumb"
                                        src={m.track.art}
                                        alt=""
                                        width="56"
                                        height="56"
                                        loading="lazy"
                                        decoding="async"
                                        referrerpolicy="no-referrer"
                                    >
                                ) : (
                                    <span class="mini-thumb--empty" aria-hidden="true">[ ]</span>
                                )}
                                <span class="mini-body" aria-hidden="true">
                                    <span class="mini-side">{m.who}</span>
                                    <span class="mini-title">{m.track.title || 'a song with no name'}</span>
                                    {m.track.note && <span class="mini-note">{m.track.note}</span>}
                                    {m.glyphs && <span class="mini-reacts">{m.glyphs}</span>}
                                </span>
                            </a>
                        ))}
                    </div>
                </li>
            ))}
        </ul>
    ) : (
        <p class="archive-empty">
            {storeUnreachable
                ? "The shelf isn’t answering. Nothing is lost."
                : `Nothing here yet. Day one, ${viewer === 'her' ? her : 'Sam'}.`}
        </p>
    )}

    <hr class="studio-rule">

    <div class="studio-foot">
        <span class="studio-tag">Love you.</span>
        <a href="/samdrea/vault">&larr; back to the room</a>
    </div>
</div>

<script is:inline define:vars={{ renderedDate: today, wingTz: WING_TZ }}>
/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Three jobs, and deliberately no more.

   Rendered unconditionally rather than wrapped in a template expression: a
   <script> inside `{...}` asks the Astro compiler to read JavaScript braces as
   template braces, and the failure mode is a build error in a file that reads
   as correct. Each block below bails out on its own if its markup is absent —
   which now happens routinely, because the reaction bar only exists on a day I
   have posted and the form only exists in her slot.

   1. REACTIONS WITHOUT A PAGE JUMP. The forms already work without this — 303,
      re-render, done. This only removes the navigation.

   2. HER POST, WITHOUT LOSING WHAT SHE TYPED. This one is not cosmetic. A
      native form submit that fails has already navigated away, and the note is
      gone with it. Everything this block buys is: on failure, the paragraph is
      still in the textarea.

   3. THE DAY ROLLOVER. Her phone will keep this tab alive for a week. Without
      this, coming back to it on Tuesday shows Monday's pair with no hint that
      it is stale.

   4. WHERE THE POSTER IS. One line, into a hidden field, so a song's time can
      later be printed on the clock of whoever posted it.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
(function () {
    var status = document.getElementById('react-status');

    /* ---- 4. where the poster is ---------------------------------------
       IT READS A ZONE NAME, NOT AN OFFSET. On iOS this is whatever Settings ▸
       General ▸ Date & Time resolved to, which is exactly the "read it from their
       iOS setting" that a source constant cannot do. It is NOT formatting
       anything: the time on the card is rendered on the SERVER, in this zone, so
       both of them read the identical string for the same song. A time formatted
       here would be formatted against whichever phone happened to be holding the
       page, and the two of them could not quote a time to each other.

       Every failure leaves the field empty and the server falls back to the
       HER_TZ / HIS_TZ constant, which is also exactly what happens with this
       script absent or blocked. There is no path where this costs a post. */
    function stampZone() {
        try {
            var here = Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone;
            if (!here) return;
            Array.prototype.forEach.call(document.querySelectorAll('input[data-tz]'), function (input) {
                input.value = here;
            });
        } catch (err) {
            /* An engine with no Intl, or one that will not name its own zone. The
               constant is the answer and the server already knows that. */
        }
    }

    /* ONCE NOW, so the field is filled whichever way the form goes out — the
       fetch below, a native submit, or a back-button resubmit. */
    stampZone();

    /* AND AGAIN THE INSTANT A FORM IS SUBMITTED, which is not belt-and-braces.
       Job 3 below exists because her phone will keep this tab alive for a WEEK,
       and the entire point of reading the zone from the device instead of a
       constant is that it follows her when she travels. A value stamped at render
       time is a value from wherever she opened the page, which for a tab that
       survives a flight is the one case this field was added to get right.

       Capture phase on the document: `submit` bubbles, so capture here runs before
       the form's own handler further down reads `form.elements.tz.value`, and it
       covers both forms and both paths from one listener. */
    document.addEventListener('submit', stampZone, true);

    /* ---- 1. reactions -------------------------------------------------

       ONLY THE TOGGLE CHIPS. `.react-any` — the free-choice input — is
       deliberately NOT enhanced and submits natively, because a successful add
       creates a chip that does not exist in this document yet. Painting it
       here would mean building markup in JavaScript that has to match the
       Astro template, which is the second-renderer problem this file refuses
       everywhere else. A 303 and a re-render is the honest answer, and it
       costs one page load on the rarer of the two actions. */
    var forms = Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('.react-form'));

    /**
     * One request at a time, globally.
     *
     * Not politeness — correctness. On the R2 storage tier every reaction is a
     * read-modify-write of one JSON object, so two overlapping taps can have the
     * second read land before the first write and silently discard it.
     * Serializing here does not fix that race (only the store could) but it
     * removes the one way this UI could reliably cause it: an impatient thumb.
     */
    var busy = false;

    /**
     * U+FE0F stripped, mirroring foldReaction() in kv.ts.
     *
     * The server answers with what it stored, which may be a bare U+2764 where
     * this button carries U+2764 U+FE0F. Comparing the raw strings would leave
     * the heart looking un-pressed immediately after a successful tap on it.
     */
    function fold(emoji) {
        return String(emoji).replace(/\uFE0F/g, '');
    }

    /** Repaint every button ON THAT SIDE from the server's answer, not from what
     *  we assumed would happen. One source of truth, even for optimism.
     *
     *  Scoped by side, which is new and necessary: both cards can carry a bar in
     *  principle, and an unscoped repaint would clear the other card's state
     *  using a list that never described it. */
    function paint(side, list) {
        var on = {};
        for (var i = 0; i < list.length; i++) on[fold(list[i])] = true;
        forms.forEach(function (form) {
            if (form.elements.side.value !== side) return;
            var button = form.querySelector('.react');
            var isOn = !!on[fold(form.elements.reaction.value)];
            button.setAttribute('aria-pressed', isOn ? 'true' : 'false');
            // The hidden field always carries the OPPOSITE, so the no-JS path
            // stays correct if she reloads after tapping.
            form.elements.on.value = isOn ? '0' : '1';
        });
    }

    function say(text) {
        if (status) status.textContent = text;
    }

    forms.forEach(function (form) {
        form.addEventListener('submit', async function (event) {
            event.preventDefault();
            if (busy) return;

            var button = form.querySelector('.react');
            var emoji = form.elements.reaction.value;
            var side = form.elements.side.value;
            /* The accessible name IS the label now: the visible caption span is
               gone from the markup, so this reads the button's aria-label. Without
               that change every announcement would have been the bare emoji. */
            var label = button.getAttribute('aria-label') || emoji;
            var want = form.elements.on.value === '1';

            busy = true;
            button.dataset.busy = '';

            var res, data;
            try {
                res = await fetch('/api/us/react', {
                    method: 'POST',
                    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
                    credentials: 'same-origin',
                    body: JSON.stringify({
                        date: form.elements.date.value,
                        side: side,
                        reaction: emoji,
                        on: want
                    })
                });
                data = await res.json();
            } catch (err) {
                say('that did not save. try again?');
                return;
            } finally {
                busy = false;
                delete button.dataset.busy;
            }

            if (!res.ok || !data || data.ok !== true) {
                /* Names the two outcomes worth naming. `own-song` should be
                   unreachable from this markup — the bar is not drawn on your own
                   card — so if it ever fires, the identity flipped in another tab
                   and a generic "not your fault" would be a lie. */
                var code = (data && data.error) || '';
                say(code === 'own-song'
                    ? "that one's yours. reactions go on the other song."
                    : code === 'too-many'
                        ? 'that song is holding all it can. take one back first?'
                        : 'that did not save. not your fault.');
                return;
            }

            paint(data.side || side, data.reactions || []);
            say(label + (want ? ': yes' : ': taken back'));
        });
    });

    /* ---- 2. her post --------------------------------------------------- */

    (function () {
        var form = document.getElementById('reply-form');
        var submit = document.getElementById('reply-submit');
        var out = document.getElementById('reply-status');
        // Bails out rather than throwing if the markup is ever absent. Everything
        // below is an enhancement over a form that already works.
        if (!form || !submit || !out) return;

        var MESSAGES = {
            'bad-url': "that isn't a spotify TRACK link — copy the link from the song itself.",
            'bad-date': 'that day is not a real day.',
            'future-date': "that day hasn't happened yet.",
            'bad-request': "the server couldn't read that.",
            unauthorized: 'your session ran out. answer the questions again and you are back in.',
            unconfigured: 'something is unplugged on my end. not your fault.',
            store: "that didn't save. nothing was posted. try again?",
            rate: 'give it a minute.'
        };

        function say(kind, text) {
            /* UNHIDDEN FIRST, THEN WRITTEN, and the order is the whole point. A
               `hidden` element is not in the accessibility tree, so several
               screen-reader/browser pairs never announce text that was already
               present at the moment the live region appeared — they only announce
               MUTATIONS to a region that is already there. Setting the text first
               and revealing second is the way to have an aria-live region that
               silently does nothing. */
            out.hidden = false;
            // The word is part of the message, so this never depends on colour.
            out.dataset.kind = kind;
            out.textContent = (kind === 'bad' ? 'hm — ' : 'posted — ') + text;
        }

        var label = submit.textContent;

        form.addEventListener('submit', async function (event) {
            event.preventDefault();

            /* Named fields, not new FormData(form) — so `tz` has to be listed here
               explicitly or the enhanced path would silently drop the very field the
               no-JavaScript path carries for free. `|| ''` because block 4 leaves it
               empty on an engine that would not name its zone, and the endpoint
               treats empty as "we do not know". */
            var body = {
                url: form.elements.url.value.trim(),
                note: form.elements.note.value,
                artist: form.elements.artist.value.trim(),
                date: form.elements.date.value,
                tz: (form.elements.tz && form.elements.tz.value) || ''
            };
            if (!body.url) {
                say('bad', 'paste a link first.');
                form.elements.url.focus();
                return;
            }

            submit.disabled = true;
            submit.textContent = 'posting';

            var res, data;
            try {
                res = await fetch('/api/us/reply', {
                    // JSON in, JSON out. The endpoint keys its response shape off
                    // this header, so the no-JS form POST still gets its redirect.
                    method: 'POST',
                    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
                    credentials: 'same-origin',
                    body: JSON.stringify(body)
                });
                data = await res.json();
            } catch (err) {
                say('bad', 'the server did not answer. nothing was posted. try again.');
                return;
            } finally {
                submit.disabled = false;
                submit.textContent = label;
            }

            if (!res.ok || !data || data.ok !== true) {
                var code = (data && data.error) || 'no';
                say('bad', MESSAGES[code] || 'that did not go through. nothing was posted.');
                return;
            }

            say('ok', data.degraded
                ? "it's up, though Spotify would not name it. The player still will."
                : "it's up. I'll see it.");

            /* Hand control back to the SERVER rather than painting her card from
               this response. Patching it here would mean a second renderer in
               JavaScript — art vs placeholder, title vs the unnamed fallback, the
               embed, the meta line — that can disagree with the Astro one, and a
               card that disagrees with the store is worse than a reload. The short
               pause is so the line above is readable first. */
            setTimeout(function () {
                window.location.assign('/samdrea/vault/today?sent=' + encodeURIComponent(data.date));
            }, 700);
        });
    })();

    /* ---- 3. the day rollover ------------------------------------------ */

    /** The same calendar rule the server used, so the two cannot disagree. */
    function clientWingDate() {
        try {
            return new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-CA', {
                timeZone: wingTz,
                year: 'numeric',
                month: '2-digit',
                day: '2-digit'
            }).format(new Date());
        } catch (err) {
            // An engine without that timezone would report a wrong date, and a
            // wrong date here means a spurious reload loop. Better to do nothing.
            return renderedDate;
        }
    }

    var checking = false;

    async function checkForNewDay() {
        if (document.hidden || checking) return;
        if (clientWingDate() === renderedDate) return;

        /* The day has changed. Ask the server what is on the shelf now — and
           note that we RELOAD rather than patch the cards in place. Patching
           would mean a second renderer in JavaScript (art vs placeholder,
           title vs the unnamed fallback, two embeds, the reaction states) that
           can disagree with the Astro one, and a page that disagrees with the
           server is worse than a flash of white. The probe is only here so a
           day with nothing posted yet does not reload her page for nothing. */
        checking = true;
        try {
            var res = await fetch('/api/us/song', {
                headers: { Accept: 'application/json' },
                credentials: 'same-origin'
            });
            if (!res.ok) return;
            var data = await res.json();
            // Reload only when the SERVER also agrees the day rolled over AND
            // there is something new to show. EITHER half counts: a new day whose
            // only content is her own song is still a page showing the wrong day.
            var pair = data && data.pair;
            if (data && data.ok && data.date !== renderedDate && pair && (pair.his || pair.hers)) {
                window.location.reload();
            }
        } catch (err) {
            /* Offline, or the store is down. Leave her page exactly as it is. */
        } finally {
            checking = false;
        }
    }

    document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', checkForNewDay);
    window.addEventListener('focus', checkForNewDay);
})();
</script>
</body>
</html>
